; ============================================================
; TITLE:    Adding Up a List
; CATEGORY: Recursion
; LEVEL:    Beginner
; TAGS:     recursion, lists, first, butfirst, sum
; SUMMARY:  Adds up a list of numbers by splitting it into its first item and the rest, and stacks colored blocks to show the running total.
; ============================================================
;
; WHAT IT DOES
;   To add up [4 7 2 9 5], take the first number, 4, and add it to the
;   total of the rest, [7 2 9 5]. The total of the rest is found the
;   same way. The total of an empty list is 0, and that is where it
;   stops. TOTAL does exactly that. TOWER draws each number as a block
;   stacked on the blocks of the rest of the list, so the height of the
;   tower is the sum.
;
; HOW TO USE
;   1. Press Run. The total is printed and the tower is drawn.
;   2. Put a breakpoint on the line
;        output (first :numbers) + total butfirst :numbers
;      and press F5 a few times. In the Variables panel :numbers gets
;      shorter each time. In the Call Stack each TOTAL is holding on to
;      a longer list.
;   3. Use Step Out (Shift+F11) to finish one TOTAL at a time and see
;      the answers come back: 5, 14, 16, 23, 27.
;
; CONCEPTS YOU WILL LEARN
;   * FIRST and BUTFIRST to take a list apart
;   * the empty list as a base case
;   * the same recursive pattern used for adding and for drawing
;
; THINGS TO TRY
;   * Change the list in the last lines to your own numbers.
;   * Write BIGGEST :numbers that outputs the largest number in a list.
;     Hint: compare the first number with the biggest of the rest.
;   * Write HOWMANY :list that counts the items without using COUNT.
; ============================================================

to total :numbers
  if emptyp :numbers [output 0]
  output (first :numbers) + total butfirst :numbers
end

; Draws the blocks from the bottom up. Each call draws one block for
; the first number, then moves up and lets the next call stack the
; rest on top. The colors cycle through the palette by list length.
to tower :numbers
  if emptyp :numbers [stop]
  block first :numbers 1 + remainder count :numbers 14
  penup fd 12 * first :numbers pendown
  tower butfirst :numbers
end

; One block, :units tall. Twelve turtle steps per unit.
to block :units :color
  localmake "h 12 * :units
  setpencolor :color
  filled :color [repeat 2 [fd :h rt 90 fd 80 rt 90]]
  setpencolor 0
  repeat 2 [fd :h rt 90 fd 80 rt 90]
  ; write the number inside the block
  penup fd :h / 2 - 5 rt 90 fd 35 lt 90
  label :units
  bk :h / 2 - 5 rt 90 bk 35 lt 90 pendown
end

to show.total :numbers
  clearscreen hideturtle
  setlabelheight 14
  print (sentence [The total of] :numbers [is] total :numbers)
  penup setxy -40 -250 setheading 0 pendown
  tower :numbers
  ; the turtle is now at the top of the tower
  penup rt 90 fd 100 lt 90 bk 8 pendown
  setpencolor 4
  label (sentence [total =] total :numbers)
end

show.total [4 7 2 9 5]
